reviews.body:
I have not reviewed the code yet but I had a chance to play with telnet and the following program and the behavior all seems reasonable to me.

```rust
extern crate serde_json;

use serde_json::Value;
use std::net::TcpListener;

fn main() {
    let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:34254").unwrap();
    let stream = listener.incoming().next().unwrap().unwrap();
    let de = serde_json::Deserializer::from_reader(stream);
    for v in de.into_iter::<Value>() {
        println!("{:?}", v.unwrap());
    }
}
```

- Could you say a bit about why you need this and how it will be used? Part of the reason for #300 was that nobody had a compelling use case for needing other types in a stream.

- Please document the new behavior thoroughly on the StreamDeserializer type. The existing comment is outdated by this change.

- Is it possible to implement this such that it only affects the codepath that involves StreamDeserializer? As far as I can tell, deserializing a single value the usual way using serde_json::from_str should not need to execute all of this new logic in parse_ident and parse_integer. It would be a shame if the only way to support this feature for StreamDeserializer meant a performance regression for users not using StreamDeserializer.
